<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=archery">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Weapons and war</p><h1 class="enc-title">Archery</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as archers</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Archery is the use of bows to shoot arrows in hunting and warfare. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mounted-archer">Mounted archers</a> were the core of steppe armies such as those of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazar Khaganate</a>, and of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthian-empire">Parthian Empire</a>, famous for its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horse-archer">horse archers</a>. Foot archers were also important in earlier states, including the Akkadian and Kushite empires, the latter known for its bowmen.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/archery.jpg" alt="Archery" width="320" height="241" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: PHGCOM · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Scythians_shooting_with_bows_Kertch_antique_Panticapeum_Ukrainia_4th_century_BCE.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under King Pratap Singha (r. 1603–1641) the official Momai Tamuli Borbarua reorganised the paiks into professional guilds called khels, which supplied the state with boatmen, <b>archers</b>, potters, goldwashers and other specialists.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ahom-kingdom">Ahom kingdom · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Sumerian warfare had relied heavily on close-order infantry, as depicted on the Standard of Ur and the Stele of the Vultures; Sargon's army appears to have made greater use of lightly equipped troops and <b>archers</b>, which allowed more flexible tactics.</blockquote><a href="/empire/akkadian-empire">Akkadian Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…the fourteenth century BCE: King Rib-Hadda wrote more than sixty letters to the pharaohs Amenhotep III and Akhenaten, pleading for <b>archers</b> and supplies as Abdi-Ashirta of Amurru and then his son Aziru seized neighbouring towns, cut off the city’s grain supply and turned its own people against him.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byblos">Byblos · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Each of its four versions reportedly took 15,000–20,000 oak and hickory logs set in a deep trench, and evenly spaced bastions allowed <b>archers</b> to shoot along the wall.</blockquote><a href="/empire/cahokia">Cahokia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Dzungar army was a mounted force of nomad <b>archers</b>, but from the late seventeenth century it also used matchlocks and, unusually for a steppe power, artillery.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dzungar-khanate">Dzungar Khanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Al-Bakri recorded that the king could field 200,000 warriors, of whom more than 40,000 were <b>archers</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ghana-empire">Ghana Empire · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>c. 80,000 years ago</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Obi-Rakhmat, Uzbekistan</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>The earliest evidence is indirect, small stone points from Uzbekistan, Sibudu in South Africa (72,000–60,000 years ago) and elsewhere; the oldest surviving bows, from Holmegaard in Denmark, date from about 9000 BCE.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/stone-tools">Stone tools<span>3.3 million years ago</span></a></div><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Led to</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/composite-bow">Composite bow<span>Bronze Age</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/crossbow">Crossbow<span>c. 650 BCE</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=archery">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
